#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-stock-take: the stock-take of milestone 1 in docs/plan.md.

Reads docs/stock-take.tsv, checks every anchor in it, computes the state of
every thing from the records and the git history, and lists every line of the
search set that no record covers. It stores nothing: the table holds facts
only, and every state is computed on every run, because a stored state is an
asserted state ("Never assert state - query it", docs/skill-conventions.md).

Usage
    scripts/devloop-stock-take                  the whole table
    scripts/devloop-stock-take --only PATH ...  coverage limited to files under
                                                the given paths
    scripts/devloop-stock-take --self-test      builds a temporary repository
                                                and produces every red outcome
                                                once, each beside its green
                                                counterpart

Exit code 0: nothing broken, nothing uncovered. 1: lines of the search set are
uncovered. 2: a record is broken, an anchor does not stand where its record
says, or the repository is one the tool refuses (a shallow clone).

It needs python3 and git and nothing else, and no GNU tool: it runs on macOS.

This tool is not one of the checks under "Before a handover, run these" in
docs/skill-conventions.md. Nearly every change to a skill touches an anchored
line, so it would answer in the ordinary case, and "Before you change
anything, run this" says what becomes of such a check: read once, explained
away, skipped. It runs where the table is used, and after every rebase of the
branch that fills the table.


A. WHAT GOES IN

- Every branch of the twelve skills, all files in each skill directory.
- Every branch of the text under shared/.
- Every outcome of each hook, and hooks.json as one thing. A hook's outcomes
  are what it produces (passes, each with the condition that produced it, or
  blocks with a given message), not its exit statements.
- Every outcome of every program under bin/ and scripts/, this tool included.
  This tool's outcomes are the cases of its self-test.
- Both outcomes of every command under "Before a handover, run these" and of
  the command under "Before you change anything, run this": the answer its
  section calls green, and any other. The green form is the record's note.
  Where the section does not say what green looks like, the note says so;
  whoever writes the record does not supply one.
- The thirteen entries under "Named, not built as skills" in docs/roadmap.md
  that the section does not record as done inside another skill or as a
  stage. The other nine are not things of their own: each name stands as a
  note on the row of the branch that does its work. The four side paths under
  "What is missing" in README.md are four of the thirteen and get no second
  row.
- Every defect an entry under "Known gaps" names, answered or not, the three
  defects and seven mechanisms of the Pocock comparison and the remaining
  points of "The order of work, and why" included. The one defect that entry
  says this set does not have, and the four places where it says this set is
  further on, name no defect of this set and get no row. Whether a defect is
  still open is computed, never judged while writing.
- Trigger, bench and cost belong to the thing, as how it could be walked, not
  to a run. Each with its anchor, where the roadmap names them.

B. WHAT A BRANCH IS

Every outcome of a condition the run reads at run time and after which it does
or says something different. Each outcome of each condition once, never each
path through several conditions. A unit with no condition at all (a step, a
section) is one row for its straight path; a sentence without a condition
inside a unit that has one is not a row. A sentence that fixes the same
wording in every case is not a branch.

Branches are read from the files on disk, never from the expanded text. A
branch in inserted text is a row of its file under shared/, once. Where the
shared text hands the decision to the skill (shared/how-to-ask.md does so for
the case with nobody there), the branch stands only at the skill.

In every skill the unattended mode reaches, every point where the skill hands
the user something to do or asks something gets a row for the case with
nobody there, even where the skill does not say what happens then. Which
skills it reaches is read from the whole of skills/build-work/SKILL.md and
from the three routes into it (start-work, plan-work, cut-into-tasks), each
with its anchor. A reached skill with neither a section of its own for the
case nor shared/how-to-ask.md inserted gets an empty evidence for that row.

A branch one skill describes and another decides stands once, where it is
decided; the other place goes in the note.

C. RECORDS

docs/stock-take.tsv: tab-separated, one record per line, the first line the
header of the columns below. No tab in any field, no leading or trailing
whitespace in an anchor, no absolute local path anywhere. Every record carries
exactly the columns of the header; a tab that an editor turned into spaces
makes the record one field short, and the record is rejected rather than read.
Fields a record type does not use stay empty; a required field that is empty,
an unknown type or kind, or a filled field the type does not use rejects the
record.

Columns, in this order:
    type name kind file anchor evidence_file evidence_anchor note date
    version commit source run_file run_anchor trigger_file trigger_anchor
    bench_file bench_anchor cost_file cost_anchor

An anchor is a file and a verbatim substring of one line of it, 20 to 80
characters, occurring exactly once in that file. An anchor may be shorter than
20 characters where it is a whole line, and it must still occur exactly once.
No line numbers anywhere: the opening of docs/plan.md records that line
numbers drifted by the next pull request while the text did not.

Three record types:

- thing: name (unique among things), kind, file + anchor (the site: where it
  stands or is named), evidence_file + evidence_anchor (what carries its
  state; may be empty), note, and optionally trigger, bench and cost, each a
  file + anchor pair. Kinds: skill branch, shared text, hook outcome, hook
  registration, program outcome, check command, named skill, defect. For a
  branch on disk, site and evidence are usually the same line. For a defect,
  the evidence is the line where its fix stands, or the line in which the
  entry itself says nothing is built, or empty. For a named skill, the note
  carries the search by what it does, not only by its name, that came back
  empty. For a check command, the note carries the green form.
- run, keyed to a thing by name: date (YYYY-MM-DD), the version it ran or the
  commit it ran, source (entry: the roadmap entry names it; log: read from the
  session log of that run; none: not recorded), run_file + run_anchor (the
  roadmap line that records the run). A commit enters a run record only where
  the entry says the run ran that commit; a commit named for another reason,
  such as the one that built a fix, is not a run's version. A run's commit
  must be an ancestor of origin/main, and the tool checks it: that rejects a
  bench's commits, which do not resolve here, and a commit that exists only on
  a branch, which a squash merge removes. A version, by contrast, is resolved
  on the history of the checkout, so a run recorded on a branch under the
  version its merge will introduce counts before the merge and after it; such
  a run is recorded only at the branch's final state.
- not-a-thing: file + anchor of a line from the search set judged not to be a
  branch, or an entry head whose entry names no defect, and a one-word reason
  in note.

D. HOW STATES ARE COMPUTED

- Undetermined: the evidence is empty.
- Recorded, not built: the evidence lies under docs/ or in README.md, and the
  kind is not check command.
- Built: the evidence lies under skills/, shared/, hooks/, bin/ or scripts/,
  or the kind is check command.
- Walked: built, and at least one of its runs counts.
- Built, never walked: built, and no run counts. Runs that do not count stay
  in the output, each with the reason.

A run counts when the version it ran already contains the last change to the
evidence line: git blame names the commit of that change, and the commit that
introduced the version number into .claude-plugin/plugin.json must have it as
an ancestor, itself included. That commit and no later one, because a
version's content is fixed only if every change to the shipped directories
raised the number; where that does not hold, it is the one commit every copy
of the version is certain to contain. The rule can undercount and cannot
overcount. Where the run carries a commit instead, that commit must have the
blamed commit as an ancestor, itself included.

Blame is plain: a moved line counts as changed, because where a line stands
is part of what it decides (a check moved to a place where its red means
something else, a rule moved onto another route). What copy detection would
attribute the line to (git blame -C -C) is printed as information beside a run
that does not count, never as a state.

A check command runs in this repository, not from the installed copy: its run
counts when the run's date is later than the commit date of that last change,
read in the commit's own timezone; the same day does not count. A run whose
version is not recorded does not count. An uncommitted line counts as changed
after every run.

On a shallow clone the tool refuses to work: git blame there reports every
line as the boundary commit.

E. THE SEARCH SET, FOR COVERAGE

- In every file under skills/ and shared/: every line holding one of the
  words if, where, when, whenever, wherever, unless, otherwise, either, else,
  before, as whole words in either case; every heading; every numbered item;
  every bold list head; every bold paragraph head; every table row except the
  header row and the separator row.
- In every file under hooks/: every line holding if, elif, else, case or exit
  as whole words.
- Under bin/ and scripts/: in a shell program the same five words; in a python
  program if, elif, raise, exit. Not else and not return: an else line on its
  own is never anchorable and its branch is anchored at its if, and a return
  is a function's straight path. The language is read off the first line.
- Under the two check headings in docs/skill-conventions.md ("Before you
  change anything, run this" and "Before a handover, run these"): every
  command block, as one item covered by an anchor on any of its lines.
- In docs/roadmap.md under "Known gaps": every entry head (a line starting
  with "- **") and every line holding "Recorded, not built", "Built",
  "Half built" or "Should hold".

A line is covered when an anchor of any record (thing, run or not-a-thing)
lies on it. A line of the set that can carry no unique anchor at all (too
short and repeated, or repeated whole) leaves the set and is listed under a
heading of its own, so it is seen rather than silently dropped; its branch is
anchored on the nearest line that can carry an anchor, normally the condition
itself. The exit lines of a hook are the case: pass and block are anchored
where they are decided.

Every order from 2 to 8 of the stock-take ends when the tool reports, for the
files that order took, no uncovered line of the set and no broken record.

LIMITS

- It proves that an anchor stands where a record says, not that it supports
  the record.
- A condition phrased without any of the search words is caught only by the
  headings, items, list heads and table rows, not with certainty.
- The version rule sees only the evidence line; a change to a neighbouring
  line of the same branch goes unseen.
- Walked, for shared text, means walked in at least one skill that inserts it.
- A version read from a session log is recorded with its source; nobody else
  can read that log again. Two of the session logs of the benches carry no
  version at all; that cannot be repaired.
- Text that moved unchanged into shared/ on 17 and 18 September 2026 counts
  for no run before it moved, although those runs received the same words.
- Coverage counts lines, not branches. A line carrying two conditions is
  covered by the first record on it.
- A defect whose fix stands on disk while its record's evidence points at the
  roadmap line saying nothing is built reads "Recorded, not built"; the tool
  cannot see the fix. Only a named skill whose directory exists is flagged.
"""

import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

COLUMNS = [
    "type", "name", "kind", "file", "anchor", "evidence_file",
    "evidence_anchor", "note", "date", "version", "commit", "source",
    "run_file", "run_anchor", "trigger_file", "trigger_anchor", "bench_file",
    "bench_anchor", "cost_file", "cost_anchor",
]
TYPES = ("thing", "run", "not-a-thing")
KINDS = (
    "skill branch", "shared text", "hook outcome", "hook registration",
    "program outcome", "check command", "named skill", "defect",
)
SOURCES = ("entry", "log", "none")
STATES = ("Undetermined", "Recorded, not built", "Built, never walked", "Walked")
SHIPPED = ("skills", "shared", "hooks", "bin", "scripts")
RECORDED_IN = ("docs", "README.md")
ZERO_HASH = "0" * 40

PROSE_WORDS = re.compile(
    r"\b(if|where|when|whenever|wherever|unless|otherwise|either|else|before)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE)
SHELL_WORDS = re.compile(r"\b(if|elif|else|case|exit)\b")
PYTHON_WORDS = re.compile(r"\b(if|elif|raise|exit)\b")
HEADING = re.compile(r"^#{1,6}\s")
NUMBERED = re.compile(r"^\s*\d+\.\s")
BOLD_LIST_HEAD = re.compile(r"^\s*[-*]\s+\*\*")
BOLD_PARA_HEAD = re.compile(r"^\*\*")
TABLE_ROW = re.compile(r"^\s*\|")
TABLE_SEPARATOR = re.compile(r"^\s*\|?\s*:?-{3,}")
ENTRY_HEAD = re.compile(r"^- \*\*")
ROADMAP_STATUS = re.compile(r"\bBuilt\b|Half built|Recorded, not built|Should hold")
DATE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
CHECK_HEADINGS = (
    "## Before you change anything, run this",
    "## Before a handover, run these",
)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- helpers

class Refused(Exception):
    """The repository is one the tool refuses to work on."""


def git(root, *args, check=True):
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args), capture_output=True,
                       text=True)
    if check and p.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError("git %s: %s" % (" ".join(args), p.stderr.strip()))
    return p.stdout


def git_ok(root, *args):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args),
                          capture_output=True, text=True).returncode == 0


def resolve(root, ref):
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify",
                        ref + "^{commit}"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    return p.stdout.strip() if p.returncode == 0 else None


def is_ancestor(root, a, b):
    """True when commit a is an ancestor of commit b, or the same commit."""
    return git_ok(root, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b)


def commit_date(root, h):
    """The committer date, in the commit's own timezone, as YYYY-MM-DD."""
    return git(root, "show", "-s", "--format=%ci", h).strip()[:10]


def blame(root, path, lineno, copy_detection=False):
    """The commit that last changed the line, or None where it is uncommitted."""
    args = ["blame", "--line-porcelain", "-L", "%d,%d" % (lineno, lineno)]
    if copy_detection:
        args += ["-C", "-C"]
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + args + ["--", path],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return None
    out = p.stdout.splitlines()
    if not out:
        return None
    h = out[0].split()[0]
    if h == ZERO_HASH:
        return None
    info = {"hash": h}
    for line in out[1:]:
        if line.startswith("filename "):
            info["filename"] = line[len("filename "):]
        elif line.startswith("committer-time "):
            info["time"] = int(line.split()[1])
    return info


def version_commit(root, version):
    """The commit that introduced the version into plugin.json, or None."""
    needle = '"version": "%s"' % version
    out = git(root, "log", "--format=%H", "-S" + needle, "--",
              ".claude-plugin/plugin.json", check=False)
    hashes = out.split()
    for h in reversed(hashes):
        shown = git(root, "show", "%s:.claude-plugin/plugin.json" % h,
                    check=False)
        if needle in shown:
            return h
    return None


def is_shallow(root):
    return git(root, "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository").strip() == "true"


class Files:
    """Every file read once."""

    def __init__(self, root):
        self.root = root
        self.cache = {}

    def get(self, rel):
        if rel not in self.cache:
            path = os.path.join(self.root, rel)
            try:
                with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    text = f.read()
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                self.cache[rel] = None
            else:
                self.cache[rel] = (text, text.splitlines())
        return self.cache[rel]


def find_anchor(files, rel, anchor):
    """(status, lineno). status: ok, missing, multiple, no-file, too-long,
    too-short, whitespace."""
    if anchor != anchor.strip():
        return "whitespace", None
    if len(anchor) > 80:
        return "too-long", None
    got = files.get(rel)
    if got is None:
        return "no-file", None
    text, lines = got
    total = text.count(anchor)
    if total == 0:
        return "missing", None
    if total > 1:
        return "multiple", None
    hits = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1) if anchor in l]
    lineno = hits[0]
    if len(anchor) < 20 and lines[lineno - 1].strip() != anchor:
        return "too-short", None
    return "ok", lineno


def anchorable(text, line):
    """Whether some substring of the line could serve as an anchor."""
    s = line.strip()
    if not s:
        return False
    if len(s) < 20:
        return text.count(s) == 1
    length = min(80, len(s))
    return any(text.count(s[i:i + length]) == 1
               for i in range(len(s) - length + 1))


def absolute_path(field):
    return field.startswith("/") or "/Users/" in field or "/home/" in field


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- records

class Record:
    def __init__(self, lineno, fields):
        self.lineno = lineno
        self.f = dict(zip(COLUMNS, fields))
        self.errors = []
        self.anchors = {}   # column pair -> (status, lineno)

    def __getitem__(self, k):
        return self.f[k]


def read_table(path):
    """(records, errors). A record with the wrong number of fields is an
    error and is not read."""
    records, errors = [], []
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", newline="") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except OSError as e:
        return [], ["cannot read %s: %s" % (path, e)]
    lines = raw.split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines.pop()
    if not lines or lines[0].rstrip("\r").split("\t") != COLUMNS:
        errors.append("line 1: the header is not the %d columns this tool "
                      "names" % len(COLUMNS))
        return [], errors
    for n, line in enumerate(lines[1:], 2):
        line = line.rstrip("\r")
        if line == "":
            continue
        fields = line.split("\t")
        if len(fields) != len(COLUMNS):
            errors.append("line %d: %d fields instead of %d; the record is "
                          "not read" % (n, len(fields), len(COLUMNS)))
            continue
        records.append(Record(n, fields))
    return records, errors


PAIRS = (("file", "anchor"), ("evidence_file", "evidence_anchor"),
         ("run_file", "run_anchor"), ("trigger_file", "trigger_anchor"),
         ("bench_file", "bench_anchor"), ("cost_file", "cost_anchor"))

USES = {
    "thing": {"name", "kind", "file", "anchor", "evidence_file",
              "evidence_anchor", "note", "trigger_file", "trigger_anchor",
              "bench_file", "bench_anchor", "cost_file", "cost_anchor"},
    "run": {"name", "note", "date", "version", "commit", "source", "run_file",
            "run_anchor"},
    "not-a-thing": {"file", "anchor", "note"},
}
REQUIRED = {
    "thing": ("name", "kind", "file", "anchor"),
    "run": ("name", "date", "source", "run_file", "run_anchor"),
    "not-a-thing": ("file", "anchor", "note"),
}


def validate(rec):
    f = rec.f
    t = f["type"]
    if t not in TYPES:
        rec.errors.append("unknown type %r" % t)
        return
    for col in COLUMNS:
        if col == "type":
            continue
        if col not in USES[t] and f[col] != "":
            rec.errors.append("%s is filled, which a %s does not use"
                              % (col, t))
    for col in REQUIRED[t]:
        if f[col] == "":
            rec.errors.append("%s is empty" % col)
    for col in COLUMNS:
        if absolute_path(f[col]):
            rec.errors.append("%s carries an absolute local path" % col)
    for a, b in PAIRS:
        if (f[a] == "") != (f[b] == ""):
            rec.errors.append("%s and %s: one is empty and the other not"
                              % (a, b))
    if t == "thing":
        if f["kind"] not in KINDS:
            rec.errors.append("unknown kind %r" % f["kind"])
        if f["kind"] in ("check command", "named skill") and f["note"] == "":
            rec.errors.append("note is empty, which a %s may not leave"
                              % f["kind"])
    if t == "run":
        if not DATE.match(f["date"]):
            rec.errors.append("date is not YYYY-MM-DD")
        if f["source"] not in SOURCES:
            rec.errors.append("source is not one of entry, log, none")
        has_v, has_c = f["version"] != "", f["commit"] != ""
        if has_v and has_c:
            rec.errors.append("both version and commit are filled")
        if f["source"] == "none" and (has_v or has_c):
            rec.errors.append("source is none but a version or commit stands")
        if f["source"] in ("entry", "log") and not (has_v or has_c):
            rec.errors.append("source is %s but neither version nor commit "
                              "stands" % f["source"])
        if has_c and f["source"] != "entry":
            rec.errors.append("a commit is a run's version only where the "
                              "entry names it (source entry)")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- analysis

class Result:
    def __init__(self):
        self.refused = None
        self.table_errors = []      # strings
        self.broken = []            # (record lineno, message)
        self.things = []            # dicts
        self.runs = []              # dicts
        self.unanchorable = []      # (file, lineno, text)
        self.uncovered = []         # (file, first, last, text)
        self.set_size = 0
        self.covered_lines = {}     # file -> set of linenos
        self.counts = {}            # (state, kind) -> n

    def broken_count(self):
        return len(self.table_errors) + len(self.broken)


def classify_location(rel, kind):
    if kind == "check command":
        return "Built"
    top = rel.split("/", 1)[0]
    if top in SHIPPED:
        return "Built"
    if top in RECORDED_IN:
        return "Recorded, not built"
    return None


def search_set(files, root, only=None):
    """Items of the search set: (file, first_line, last_line, text)."""
    items = []

    def wanted(rel):
        return not only or any(rel == o or rel.startswith(o.rstrip("/") + "/")
                               for o in only)

    def walk(top):
        out = []
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(os.path.join(root, top)):
            dirnames.sort()
            for fn in sorted(filenames):
                out.append(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dirpath, fn), root))
        return out

    for top in ("skills", "shared"):
        for rel in walk(top):
            if not rel.endswith(".md") or not wanted(rel):
                continue
            got = files.get(rel)
            if got is None:
                continue
            lines = got[1]
            for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
                if TABLE_ROW.match(l):
                    nxt = lines[i] if i < len(lines) else ""
                    if TABLE_SEPARATOR.match(l) or TABLE_SEPARATOR.match(nxt):
                        continue
                    items.append((rel, i, i, l))
                elif (PROSE_WORDS.search(l) or HEADING.match(l)
                      or NUMBERED.match(l) or BOLD_LIST_HEAD.match(l)
                      or BOLD_PARA_HEAD.match(l)):
                    items.append((rel, i, i, l))
    for top in ("hooks", "bin", "scripts"):
        for rel in walk(top):
            if not wanted(rel):
                continue
            got = files.get(rel)
            if got is None:
                continue
            lines = got[1]
            words = SHELL_WORDS
            if top != "hooks" and lines and "python" in lines[0]:
                words = PYTHON_WORDS
            for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
                if words.search(l):
                    items.append((rel, i, i, l))
    rel = "docs/skill-conventions.md"
    if wanted(rel) and files.get(rel) is not None:
        lines = files.get(rel)[1]
        inside = False
        start = None
        for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if l.startswith("## "):
                inside = l.rstrip() in CHECK_HEADINGS
            if inside and l.startswith("    "):
                if start is None:
                    start = i
            else:
                if start is not None:
                    items.append((rel, start, i - 1, lines[start - 1]))
                    start = None
        if start is not None:
            items.append((rel, start, len(lines), lines[start - 1]))
    rel = "docs/roadmap.md"
    if wanted(rel) and files.get(rel) is not None:
        lines = files.get(rel)[1]
        inside = False
        for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if l.startswith("## "):
                inside = l.rstrip() == "## Known gaps"
                continue
            if inside and (ENTRY_HEAD.match(l) or ROADMAP_STATUS.search(l)):
                items.append((rel, i, i, l))
    return items


def analyse(root, table=None, only=None):
    res = Result()
    if table is None:
        table = os.path.join(root, "docs", "stock-take.tsv")
    try:
        if is_shallow(root):
            res.refused = ("shallow clone: git blame reports every line as "
                           "the boundary commit, so nothing here can be "
                           "computed")
            return res
    except RuntimeError as e:
        res.refused = str(e)
        return res
    files = Files(root)
    records, res.table_errors = read_table(table)
    for rec in records:
        validate(rec)
    # anchors
    for rec in records:
        for a, b in PAIRS:
            if rec[a] == "":
                continue
            status, lineno = find_anchor(files, rec[a], rec[b])
            rec.anchors[b] = (status, lineno)
            if status != "ok":
                rec.errors.append("%s %r in %s: %s" % (b, rec[b], rec[a], {
                    "missing": "not found",
                    "multiple": "found more than once, so it is not an anchor",
                    "no-file": "the file cannot be read",
                    "too-long": "longer than 80 characters",
                    "too-short": "shorter than 20 characters and not a whole line",
                    "whitespace": "carries leading or trailing whitespace",
                }[status]))
            else:
                res.covered_lines.setdefault(rec[a], set()).add(lineno)
    # duplicates among things
    seen_anchor, seen_name = {}, {}
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "thing":
            continue
        key = (rec["file"], rec["anchor"])
        if key in seen_anchor:
            rec.errors.append("same site anchor as the thing on line %d"
                              % seen_anchor[key])
        else:
            seen_anchor[key] = rec.lineno
        if rec["name"] in seen_name:
            rec.errors.append("same name as the thing on line %d"
                              % seen_name[rec["name"]])
        else:
            seen_name[rec["name"]] = rec.lineno
        if rec["kind"] == "named skill" and os.path.isdir(
                os.path.join(root, "skills", rec["name"])):
            rec.errors.append("named skill, but skills/%s exists"
                              % rec["name"])
    # runs need their thing
    things_by_name = {r["name"]: r for r in records
                      if r["type"] == "thing" and not r.errors}
    origin_main = resolve(root, "origin/main")
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "run":
            continue
        if rec["name"] not in things_by_name:
            rec.errors.append("no thing named %r" % rec["name"])
        if rec["commit"] != "" and not rec.errors:
            h = resolve(root, rec["commit"])
            if h is None:
                rec.errors.append("commit %s does not resolve here"
                                  % rec["commit"])
            elif origin_main is None:
                rec.errors.append("origin/main is not here, so the commit "
                                  "cannot be checked against it")
            elif not is_ancestor(root, h, origin_main):
                rec.errors.append("commit %s is not an ancestor of origin/main"
                                  % rec["commit"])
    for rec in records:
        for e in rec.errors:
            res.broken.append((rec.lineno, e))
    # states
    things = {}
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "thing" or rec.errors:
            continue
        t = {"rec": rec, "name": rec["name"], "kind": rec["kind"],
             "site": (rec["file"], rec.anchors["anchor"][1]),
             "evidence": None, "state": None, "runs": []}
        if rec["evidence_file"] != "":
            t["evidence"] = (rec["evidence_file"],
                             rec.anchors["evidence_anchor"][1])
            loc = classify_location(rec["evidence_file"], rec["kind"])
            if loc is None:
                res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "evidence under %s is neither "
                                   "a shipped directory nor docs/ or README.md"
                                   % rec["evidence_file"]))
                continue
            t["state"] = loc
        else:
            t["state"] = "Undetermined"
        things[t["name"]] = t
        res.things.append(t)
    # runs
    for rec in records:
        if rec["type"] != "run" or rec.errors:
            continue
        t = things.get(rec["name"])
        if t is None:
            continue
        r = {"rec": rec, "thing": t, "counts": False, "reason": "", "info": ""}
        t["runs"].append(r)
        res.runs.append(r)
        if t["state"] != "Built":
            res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "a run on %r, which is %s and "
                               "cannot be walked" % (t["name"], t["state"])))
            r["reason"] = "the thing is not built"
            continue
        efile, eline = t["evidence"]
        b = blame(root, efile, eline)
        if b is None:
            r["reason"] = "the evidence line is uncommitted"
            continue
        change_date = commit_date(root, b["hash"])
        if t["kind"] == "check command":
            if rec["date"] > change_date:
                r["counts"] = True
            else:
                r["reason"] = ("a check command's run counts only after the "
                               "day of the last change, %s at %s"
                               % (change_date, b["hash"][:7]))
        elif rec["commit"] != "":
            h = resolve(root, rec["commit"])
            if is_ancestor(root, b["hash"], h):
                r["counts"] = True
            else:
                r["reason"] = ("the run's commit %s does not contain the last "
                               "change to the evidence line, %s at %s"
                               % (rec["commit"], change_date, b["hash"][:7]))
        elif rec["version"] != "":
            vc = version_commit(root, rec["version"])
            if vc is None:
                res.broken.append((rec.lineno, "version %s was never "
                                   "introduced into .claude-plugin/plugin.json "
                                   "on this history" % rec["version"]))
                r["reason"] = "the version is not on this history"
            elif is_ancestor(root, b["hash"], vc):
                r["counts"] = True
            else:
                r["reason"] = ("version %s (introduced at %s) predates the "
                               "last change to the evidence line, %s at %s"
                               % (rec["version"], vc[:7], change_date,
                                  b["hash"][:7]))
        else:
            r["reason"] = "the version of the run is not recorded"
        if not r["counts"] and b is not None:
            c = blame(root, efile, eline, copy_detection=True)
            if c is not None and c["hash"] != b["hash"]:
                r["info"] = ("copy detection attributes the line to %s (%s) "
                             "in %s" % (c["hash"][:7],
                                        commit_date(root, c["hash"]),
                                        c.get("filename", efile)))
    for t in res.things:
        if t["state"] == "Built":
            t["state"] = ("Walked" if any(r["counts"] for r in t["runs"])
                          else "Built, never walked")
        key = (t["state"], t["kind"])
        res.counts[key] = res.counts.get(key, 0) + 1
    # coverage
    items = search_set(files, root, only)
    for rel, first, last, text in items:
        got = files.get(rel)
        if first == last and not anchorable(got[0], text):
            res.unanchorable.append((rel, first, text))
            continue
        res.set_size += 1
        covered = res.covered_lines.get(rel, set())
        if not any(first <= n <= last for n in covered):
            res.uncovered.append((rel, first, last, text))
    return res


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- output

def render(res):
    out = []
    if res.refused:
        out.append("REFUSED: " + res.refused)
        return "\n".join(out) + "\n"
    out.append("BROKEN RECORDS: %d" % res.broken_count())
    for e in res.table_errors:
        out.append("  " + e)
    for lineno, e in res.broken:
        out.append("  line %d: %s" % (lineno, e))
    out.append("")
    out.append("STATES, by file and kind (evidence that decided each)")
    groups = {}
    for t in res.things:
        gfile = t["evidence"][0] if t["evidence"] else t["site"][0]
        groups.setdefault((gfile, t["kind"]), []).append(t)
    for (gfile, kind) in sorted(groups):
        out.append("  %s / %s" % (gfile, kind))
        for t in sorted(groups[(gfile, kind)], key=lambda t: t["name"]):
            ev = ("%s:%d" % t["evidence"]) if t["evidence"] else "no evidence"
            out.append("    %-22s %s  (%s)" % (t["state"], t["name"], ev))
    out.append("")
    out.append("RUNS THAT DO NOT COUNT")
    n = 0
    for r in res.runs:
        if r["counts"]:
            continue
        n += 1
        rec = r["rec"]
        out.append("  line %d, %s on %s: %s" % (rec.lineno, rec["date"],
                                                 r["thing"]["name"], r["reason"]))
        if r["info"]:
            out.append("    " + r["info"])
    if n == 0:
        out.append("  none")
    out.append("")
    out.append("COUNTS per state and kind")
    for state in STATES:
        total = sum(v for (s, k), v in res.counts.items() if s == state)
        out.append("  %-22s %d" % (state, total))
        for kind in KINDS:
            v = res.counts.get((state, kind), 0)
            if v:
                out.append("    %-20s %d" % (kind, v))
    out.append("")
    out.append("LINES OF THE SEARCH SET THAT CAN CARRY NO UNIQUE ANCHOR "
               "(they leave the set): %d" % len(res.unanchorable))
    for rel, lineno, text in res.unanchorable:
        out.append("  %s:%d: %s" % (rel, lineno, text.strip()[:70]))
    out.append("")
    out.append("UNCOVERED LINES OF THE SEARCH SET: %d of %d"
               % (len(res.uncovered), res.set_size))
    for rel, first, last, text in res.uncovered:
        where = "%d" % first if first == last else "%d-%d" % (first, last)
        out.append("  %s:%s: %s" % (rel, where, text.strip()[:70]))
    return "\n".join(out) + "\n"


def exit_code(res):
    if res.refused or res.broken_count():
        return 2
    if res.uncovered:
        return 1
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- self-test

def selftest():
    """Builds a temporary repository and produces every red outcome once,
    each beside its green counterpart. Prints one line per case."""
    failures = []

    def case(name, red, green):
        ok = red and green
        print("%s  %s: red %s, green %s" % ("ok " if ok else "BAD", name,
                                            "seen" if red else "MISSING",
                                            "seen" if green else "MISSING"))
        if not ok:
            failures.append(name)

    def run_git(root, *args, date=None):
        env = dict(os.environ)
        env.update({"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "t", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "t@t",
                    "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "t", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "t@t"})
        if date:
            env["GIT_AUTHOR_DATE"] = date
            env["GIT_COMMITTER_DATE"] = date
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args), env=env,
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            raise RuntimeError("git %s: %s" % (" ".join(args), p.stderr))
        return p.stdout.strip()

    def write(root, rel, text):
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(text)

    def plugin(v):
        return '{\n  "name": "t",\n  "version": "%s",\n  "skills": []\n}\n' % v

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        root = os.path.join(tmp, "repo")
        os.makedirs(root)
        run_git(root, "init", "-q", "-b", "main")
        write(root, ".claude-plugin/plugin.json", plugin("0.1.0"))
        write(root, "skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
              "# Alpha\n\nIf the sky is blue, say that it is blue today.\n"
              "Where the sky is grey, say nothing about the weather.\n"
              "Plain prose that decides nothing at all here.\n"
              "Unless it rains, carry on with the ordinary work.\n")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(root, "skills", "beta"))
        write(root, "skills/beta/SKILL.md", "# Beta\n")
        write(root, "shared/note.md",
              "When somebody writes in German, answer in German.\n")
        write(root, "hooks/guard.sh",
              "#!/bin/sh\n[ -d docs/agents ] || exit 0\n"
              "echo blocked >&2\nexit 2\nexit 0\nexit 0\n")
        write(root, "docs/skill-conventions.md",
              "# Conventions\n\n## Before a handover, run these\n\n"
              "One check, silent when green:\n\n"
              "    ls skills/ | grep -c nothing-of-that-name\n\n"
              "## Something else\n\nText.\n")
        write(root, "docs/roadmap.md",
              "# Roadmap\n\n## Known gaps\n\n"
              "- **A defect measured on 2026-01-06, on the first version.**\n"
              "  Recorded, not built.\n"
              "- **A run of alpha on 2026-01-11, on the second version.**\n"
              "- **A run of the check on the day of the change.**\n"
              "- **A run of the check the day after.**\n"
              "- **A run of alpha without a version.**\n"
              "- **A run on the shared note.**\n"
              "- **A run that names a commit of main.**\n"
              "- **A run that names a commit of a side branch.**\n\n"
              "## Decisions\n\nNone.\n")
        run_git(root, "add", "-A")
        run_git(root, "commit", "-q", "-m", "c1", date="2026-01-01T12:00:00+0100")
        c1 = run_git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
        # c2 changes the evidence line of alpha
        write(root, "skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
              "# Alpha\n\nIf the sky is blue, say that it is blue today.\n"
              "Where the sky is grey, say that it is grey and stop.\n"
              "Plain prose that decides nothing at all here.\n"
              "Unless it rains, carry on with the ordinary work.\n")
        run_git(root, "add", "-A")
        run_git(root, "commit", "-q", "-m", "c2", date="2026-01-05T12:00:00+0100")
        c2 = run_git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
        # c3 raises the version
        write(root, ".claude-plugin/plugin.json", plugin("0.2.0"))
        run_git(root, "add", "-A")
        run_git(root, "commit", "-q", "-m", "c3", date="2026-01-10T12:00:00+0100")
        c3 = run_git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
        run_git(root, "update-ref", "refs/remotes/origin/main", c3)
        # c4 on a side branch, never merged
        run_git(root, "checkout", "-q", "-b", "side")
        write(root, "shared/side.md", "Either way, this stays on the side.\n")
        run_git(root, "add", "-A")
        run_git(root, "commit", "-q", "-m", "c4", date="2026-01-12T12:00:00+0100")
        c4 = run_git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
        run_git(root, "checkout", "-q", "main")
        # an uncommitted change to the shared note
        write(root, "shared/note.md",
              "When somebody writes in German, answer in German, always.\n")

        def rec(**kw):
            return "\t".join(kw.get(c, "") for c in COLUMNS)

        rows = [
            "\t".join(COLUMNS),
            # things
            rec(type="thing", name="alpha-grey", kind="skill branch",
                file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                anchor="Where the sky is grey, say that it is grey",
                evidence_file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                evidence_anchor="Where the sky is grey, say that it is grey"),
            rec(type="thing", name="alpha-blue", kind="skill branch",
                file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                anchor="If the sky is blue, say that it is blue",
                evidence_file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                evidence_anchor="If the sky is blue, say that it is blue"),
            rec(type="thing", name="note-german", kind="shared text",
                file="shared/note.md",
                anchor="When somebody writes in German",
                evidence_file="shared/note.md",
                evidence_anchor="When somebody writes in German"),
            rec(type="thing", name="check-one", kind="check command",
                file="docs/skill-conventions.md",
                anchor="ls skills/ | grep -c nothing-of-that-name",
                evidence_file="docs/skill-conventions.md",
                evidence_anchor="ls skills/ | grep -c nothing-of-that-name",
                note="green prints nothing"),
            rec(type="thing", name="guard-block", kind="hook outcome",
                file="hooks/guard.sh", anchor="echo blocked >&2",
                evidence_file="hooks/guard.sh", evidence_anchor="echo blocked >&2"),
            rec(type="thing", name="a-defect", kind="defect",
                file="docs/roadmap.md",
                anchor="A defect measured on 2026-01-06",
                evidence_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                evidence_anchor="Recorded, not built."),
            # red: anchor missing
            rec(type="thing", name="missing-anchor", kind="skill branch",
                file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                anchor="this text stands nowhere in the file"),
            # red: anchor found twice (a short whole line that repeats)
            rec(type="thing", name="double-anchor", kind="hook outcome",
                file="hooks/guard.sh", anchor="exit 0"),
            # red: two things on one anchor
            rec(type="thing", name="same-anchor-twin", kind="skill branch",
                file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                anchor="If the sky is blue, say that it is blue"),
            # red: named skill whose directory exists; green: one that does not
            rec(type="thing", name="beta", kind="named skill",
                file="docs/roadmap.md", anchor="A run of alpha without a version",
                note="grep -rn beta skills/ came back empty"),
            rec(type="thing", name="gamma", kind="named skill",
                file="docs/roadmap.md", anchor="A run on the shared note",
                note="grep -rn gamma skills/ came back empty"),
            # runs
            rec(type="run", name="alpha-grey", date="2026-01-06", version="0.1.0",
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A defect measured on 2026-01-06, on the first"),
            rec(type="run", name="alpha-grey", date="2026-01-11", version="0.2.0",
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run of alpha on 2026-01-11, on the second"),
            rec(type="run", name="alpha-blue", date="2026-01-11", source="none",
                run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run of alpha without a version"),
            rec(type="run", name="check-one", date="2026-01-01", version="0.1.0",
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run of the check on the day of the change"),
            rec(type="run", name="check-one", date="2026-01-02", version="0.1.0",
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run of the check the day after"),
            rec(type="run", name="note-german", date="2026-01-11", version="0.2.0",
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run on the shared note"),
            rec(type="run", name="guard-block", date="2026-01-11", commit=c2[:7],
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run that names a commit of main"),
            rec(type="run", name="guard-block", date="2026-01-13", commit=c4[:7],
                source="entry", run_file="docs/roadmap.md",
                run_anchor="A run that names a commit of a side branch"),
            # not-a-thing
            rec(type="not-a-thing", file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                anchor="Unless it rains, carry on", note="prose"),
            # red: a record whose first tab became spaces (19 fields)
            rec(type="thing", name="tab-lost", kind="skill branch",
                file="skills/alpha/SKILL.md",
                anchor="Plain prose that decides").replace("\t", "    ", 1),
        ]
        table = os.path.join(root, "docs", "stock-take.tsv")
        write(root, "docs/stock-take.tsv", "\n".join(rows) + "\n")
        res = analyse(root, table)
        print(render(res))
        errors = [e for _, e in res.broken] + res.table_errors
        runs = {(r["thing"]["name"], r["rec"]["date"]): r for r in res.runs}
        states = {t["name"]: t["state"] for t in res.things}

        def err(sub):
            return any(sub in e for e in errors)

        case("anchor missing",
             err("'this text stands nowhere in the file' in skills/alpha/SKILL.md: not found"),
             states.get("alpha-blue") is not None)
        case("anchor found twice",
             err("'exit 0' in hooks/guard.sh: found more than once"),
             states.get("guard-block") is not None)
        case("two thing records with one anchor",
             err("same site anchor as the thing on line"),
             states.get("alpha-grey") is not None)
        case("run whose version predates the last change",
             not runs[("alpha-grey", "2026-01-06")]["counts"]
             and "predates" in runs[("alpha-grey", "2026-01-06")]["reason"],
             runs[("alpha-grey", "2026-01-11")]["counts"]
             and states["alpha-grey"] == "Walked")
        case("run with no recorded version",
             not runs[("alpha-blue", "2026-01-11")]["counts"]
             and "not recorded" in runs[("alpha-blue", "2026-01-11")]["reason"]
             and states["alpha-blue"] == "Built, never walked",
             runs[("alpha-grey", "2026-01-11")]["counts"])
        case("check-command run on the day of the change",
             not runs[("check-one", "2026-01-01")]["counts"],
             runs[("check-one", "2026-01-02")]["counts"]
             and states["check-one"] == "Walked")
        case("uncommitted evidence line",
             not runs[("note-german", "2026-01-11")]["counts"]
             and "uncommitted" in runs[("note-german", "2026-01-11")]["reason"],
             runs[("guard-block", "2026-01-11")]["counts"])
        uncovered = {(f, a) for f, a, b, _ in res.uncovered}
        case("a line of the set nobody covers",
             ("skills/alpha/SKILL.md", 1) in uncovered,
             ("skills/alpha/SKILL.md", 6) not in uncovered
             and ("skills/alpha/SKILL.md", 3) not in uncovered)
        case("named skill whose directory exists",
             err("named skill, but skills/beta exists"),
             states.get("gamma") == "Undetermined"
             and not err("skills/gamma exists"))
        case("record with a wrong number of fields",
             any("19 fields instead of 20" in e for e in res.table_errors),
             states.get("a-defect") == "Recorded, not built")
        unanch = {(f, n) for f, n, _ in res.unanchorable}
        case("a line of the set that can carry no unique anchor, listed",
             ("hooks/guard.sh", 5) in unanch and ("hooks/guard.sh", 6) in unanch,
             ("hooks/guard.sh", 4) not in unanch
             and ("hooks/guard.sh", 2) not in unanch)
        case("run whose commit is not an ancestor of origin/main",
             err("commit %s is not an ancestor of origin/main" % c4[:7]),
             runs[("guard-block", "2026-01-11")]["counts"])
        # shallow clone
        shallow = os.path.join(tmp, "shallow")
        subprocess.run(["git", "clone", "-q", "--depth", "1", "file://" + root,
                        shallow], check=True, capture_output=True)
        res_shallow = analyse(shallow, table)
        case("shallow clone",
             res_shallow.refused is not None and "shallow" in res_shallow.refused,
             res.refused is None)
    if failures:
        print("SELF-TEST FAILED: " + ", ".join(failures))
        return 2
    print("SELF-TEST PASSED: 13 cases, every red outcome once, every green "
          "counterpart once")
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- main

def main(argv):
    if "--self-test" in argv:
        return selftest()
    only = []
    if "--only" in argv:
        only = argv[argv.index("--only") + 1:]
        if not only:
            print("--only needs at least one path", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    root = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
    res = analyse(root, only=only)
    sys.stdout.write(render(res))
    return exit_code(res)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
